<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfred Russel Wallace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1823">1823</a>–<a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfred Russel Wallace was an English naturalist and explorer, born in Llanbadoc, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monmouthshire">Monmouthshire</a>, who independently conceived the theory of evolution through natural selection. His 1858 paper on the subject was published alongside extracts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a>'s writings, which spurred Darwin to write the abstract published in 1859 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/on-the-origin-of-species">On the Origin of Species</a>. After fieldwork in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> River basin he worked in the Malay Archipelago, where he identified the faunal divide now called the Wallace Line, and he is sometimes called the father of biogeography. He also became a socialist and an advocate of spiritualism, and described his travels in The Malay Archipelago (1869).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfred-russel-wallace.jpg" alt="Alfred Russel Wallace" width="330" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: London Stereoscopic and Photographic Company (active 1855-1922) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfred-Russel-Wallace-c1895.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 18 June 1858 Darwin received from <b>Alfred Russel Wallace</b>, then in the Maluku Islands, twenty pages describing a mechanism similar to his own.</blockquote><a href="/work/on-the-origin-of-species">On the Origin of Species · How It Was Made</a></li></ul></section>
